+# Native Synergy 3 compatibility
+
+Native Synergy 3.8.4 requests RemoteDesktop sessions with an empty app ID on the tested Hyprland system. The fallback verifies the session's unique D-Bus sender, resolves that sender's PID through the session bus, and reads `/proc/<pid>/exe`. Only the exact installed path `/opt/Synergy/synergy-core` is accepted. Unknown app IDs, arbitrary executables, failed lookups and truncated paths remain denied. Existing frontend-owner and session-owner checks remain in force. This assumes the installation under `/opt/Synergy` is managed by the administrator and not writable by an untrusted user.
+
+## Optional service override
+
+The shipped systemd unit's `PrivateTmp`, `ProtectSystem` and `ProtectHome` settings place the process in a namespace where the tested user service cannot resolve the native caller's `/proc/<pid>/exe`. A manually launched backend can succeed while the D-Bus activated unit denies the same caller.
+
+`data/synergy-caller-verification.conf.example` is an opt-in workaround for that situation. It is deliberately not installed automatically. Copy it to `~/.config/systemd/user/hypr-kdeconnect-portal.service.d/caller-verification.conf`, reload the user manager, and restart the bridge during a planned pause in input sharing. It disables filesystem isolation for this service; `NoNewPrivileges`, address-family restrictions and the other unit protections remain. Do not apply it merely to suppress an unrelated authorization failure. A design that preserves filesystem isolation while providing verifiable caller credentials would be preferable upstream.
+
+## Clipboard
+
+This bridge does not implement the portal Clipboard interface. Advertising clipboard support without implementing it would be incorrect. A separate, opt-in Wayland clipboard companion can share the regular clipboard and primary selection over an independently authenticated encrypted connection.
+
+## Credits
+
+This work builds on [iamnarayana/wayland-kdeconnect-fix](https://github.com/iamnarayana/wayland-kdeconnect-fix), its original upstream project, and the [Omarchy community's Synergy/Deskflow guide](https://github.com/omacom/omarchy/discussions/10097). The existing MIT license and attribution are retained. Changes were prepared with OpenAI Codex (GPT-6); hardware acceptance was performed by the system owner.
